 "text": "Freelancer sounds modern. It actually means a soldier for hire, with a lance. Mercenaries fought for whoever paid them. But the word itself? Dictionaries trace it to a novel, Ivanhoe, by Sir Walter Scott, around 1820. A lord brags about his hired knights, his freelances. Free because they weren't loyal to any lord. Later it meant independent politicians. Then, anyone who works for themselves. Every legend has a beginning.",
